{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_243665182#7_552714236","relevance":3,"reason":"Dedicated overview of Mexican Muralism with direct evidence that Rivera aimed to make “the masses the hero of monumental art” and that murals told histories of labor, industry, and society, directly supporting social-structure influence.","learned":"Mexican muralism affected social hierarchy by placing workers, peasants, and the masses at the center of monumental public art rather than elites."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q147","reason":"The current query continues to return grade-3 evidence about muralism, class, labor, and social identity; continue harvesting relevant results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q147"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_57_1342511605#4_2485333648","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q147","rank":4,"snippet":"People Mural - The Characteristics and Significance Los Tres Grandes - The Three Great Muralists of Mexico Diego Rivera José Clemente Orozco David Alfaro Siqueiros Video - Most Important Mexican Muralists of the Muralist Arts Movement The <cover>Influence and Legacy Follow These Artists Follow These Galleries \" , \"segment\": \"Their communist backgrounds and the respect for Marxism and class struggle, however, were often visible in their murals, although always subtly and never quite radically. José Clemente Orozco - Destruction of the Old Order (detail), 1926 at National Preparatory School, Mexico City Los Tres Grandes - The Three Great Muralists of Mexico We wouldn’t be able to talk about Mexican muralism as we know it today without its three key muralists: Diego Rivera, José Clemente Orozco and David Alfaro Siqueiros. Legendary, innovative, daring and incredibly patriotic, these gentlemen were “los tres grandes” - the three great ones who had continued the work of José Vasconcelos which used art as the most powerful tool in cultural and social </cover>revolution. With various styles and temperaments, yet with the same visions, los tres grandes all graduated from the prestigious San Carlos Academy, studied in Europe and were inspired by its modernist painting, although in different "}}
