{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q147"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_57_1342511605#1_2485324887","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q147","rank":6,"snippet":"Grandes - The Three Great Muralists of Mexico Diego Rivera José Clemente Orozco David Alfaro Siqueiros Video - Most Important Mexican Muralists of the Muralist Arts Movement The <cover>Influence and Legacy Follow These Artists Follow These Galleries \" , \"segment\": \"indeed, it is probably safe to say that no other movement proposed and produced art for the people quite like the Mexican mural painting, intertwined with its nation and tradition with such dedication and vision. Diego Rivera - La historia de México- de la conquista al futuro, 1929-1935. Mural at Palacio Nacional, Ciudad de México Mexican Muralism - Hand-in-Hand with Politics and People After the Mexican Revolution, which had dictator Porfirio Díaz and his regime overthrown, and the political turmoil that followed for the next few years, Mexico saw the creation of a new party, the Partido Revolucionario Institucional - the PRI. The land was finally in the hands of its own workers, but there was so much to be done: the new government sought to establish a brand new Mexican society, </cover>founded on its rich tradition but very much forward-looking. Since most of the population was illiterate, an effective way to change that would be through "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"5/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_57_1342511605#1_2485324887","relevance":3,"reason":"Dedicated Mexican muralism article with direct evidence that muralism was 'art for the people,' tied to post-revolution politics, land in workers' hands, and the government's effort to build a new Mexican society for a largely illiterate population.","learned":"Mexican murals influenced social structure by serving as public, accessible political education and helping construct a new worker-centered post-revolutionary society."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q147","reason":"This query continues to return grade-3 evidence; proceed to the next result while it remains productive."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q147"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_57_1342511605#0_2485322181","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q147","rank":7,"snippet":"Atl and the creator of the first Modern mural. These highly educated and cultured men were of immense <cover>influence on an entire generation of Mexican muralists that continued their work a decade later all the way through the 1970s. It was an uprising that let art speak in its name; indeed, it is probably safe to say that no other movement proposed and produced art for the people quite like the Mexican mural painting, intertwined with its nation and tradition with such dedication and vision. Diego Rivera - La historia de México- de la conquista al futuro, 1929-1935. Mural at Palacio Nacional, Ciudad de México Mexican Muralism - Hand-in-Hand with Politics and People After the Mexican Revolution, which had dictator Porfirio Díaz and his regime overthrown, and the political turmoil that followed for the next few years, Mexico saw the creation of a new party, the Partido Revolucionario Institucional - the PRI. The land was finally in the hands of its own workers, but there was so much to be done: the new government sought to establish a brand new Mexican society, </cover>founded on its rich tradition but very much forward-looking.\" , \"start_char\": "}}
