{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_57_1342511605#0_2485322181","relevance":3,"reason":"Dedicated Mexican muralism article with direct evidence that the movement produced 'art for the people' and was tied to post-revolution efforts to establish a new Mexican society with land in workers' hands.","learned":"Additional support that Mexican muralism served post-revolution social reconstruction by centering workers, nation, tradition, and public art."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q147","reason":"The query remains highly productive for direct evidence on Mexican muralism's effect on society, class, and public political identity."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q147"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_105840807#9_118800273","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q147","rank":8,"snippet":"<cover>Political expression Influence Women of Mexican muralism Aurora Reyes Flores, first woman muralist Elena Huerta Muzquiz, artist of the biggest mural created by a woman in Mexico Rina Lazo, Rivera's assistant for 10 years until his death, his \"right hand\" See also Further reading References External links \" , \"segment\": \"However, most of the murals in the Escuela National Preparatoria were done by José Clemente Orozco with themes of a mestizo Mexico, the ideas of renovation and the tragedies of the Revolution; Fernando Leal painted [\\\"dancers of Chalma\\\"] \\\"Los danzantes de Chalma\\\", no earlier than 1922. Opposite that mural, Jean Charlot painted La conquista de Tenochtitlán (Conquest of Tenochtitlan) by Jean Charlot—invited by Leal. After the mural project at the National Preparatory High School This project at the institution was followed by projects at the Palacio Nacional, the interior walls of the Palacio de Bellas Artes, the Escuela Nacional de Chapingo the Escuela Nacional de Medicina and the Secretaria de Educación Pública building among many others. Ramón Alva de la Canal, Mural at Colegio San Ildefonso The movement was strongest from the 1920s to the 1950s, which corresponded to the country's transformation from </cover>"}}
