{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_00_412866569#1_757777635","relevance":3,"reason":"Dedicated art history teaching resource on Mexican muralism with direct evidence that the movement was government-funded public art used after the Revolution to transform a divided Mexico into a coherent nation and communicate a shared official history to a poor, illiterate populace.","learned":"Mexican muralism affected social structure by functioning as public nation-building and civic education for poor and illiterate citizens, helping unify post-revolutionary society."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q149","reason":"This query is focused on a strong social-structure effect—public education and nation-building—so continue to find more grade-3 documents with similar direct evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q149"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_243665182#4_552708014","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q149","rank":3,"snippet":"composer with genius enough to give it communicable form.\" 11 of 11 Summary of <cover>Mexican Muralism Originally spawned by the need to promote pride and nationalism in a country rebuilding after revolution, the Mexican Muralist movement brought mural painting back from its staid retirement in the history of ancient peoples as a respected artistic form with a strong social potential. With it, a rich visual language emerged in public spaces as a means to make art accessible to all. It provided an opportunity to educate and inform the common man with its messages of cultural identity, politics, oppression, resistance, progress, and other important issues of the time. It was a fiercely independent movement; many of its early artists rejecting external influences and used this new, vast, and freeing medium to achieve personal expression. This movement proved that art could be a valid communication tool outside the confines of the gallery and museum. Key Ideas & Accomplishments Murals were originally used as a way to spread visual messages to an illiterate population, which opened up new possibilities in the inclusion and cohesiveness of community within a people. </cover>Oftentimes these messages promoted pride in cultural identity, rich historical traditions, or political propaganda. The ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_00_412866569#2_757780181","rank":2,"relevance":3}]}}
{"request":{"judgments":"14/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_243665182#4_552708014","relevance":3,"reason":"Dedicated Mexican Muralism overview with direct evidence that the movement promoted pride and nationalism, made art accessible to all, educated the common man about cultural identity, politics, oppression and resistance, and spread visual messages to an illiterate population to increase community inclusion and cohesiveness.","learned":"Mexican murals influenced social structure by using public art to educate and unify ordinary and illiterate people, promote cultural identity, and make political/social issues accessible outside elite galleries."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q149","reason":"The query is yielding strong grade-3 evidence about muralism's public education, inclusion, and nation-building effects, so continue to the next result."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q149"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_14_1175329779#0_2438374238","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q149","rank":4,"snippet":"Key Takeaways Key Points Key Terms Mexican Muralism Los Tres Grandes Diego Rivera Jose Clemente Orozco David Alfaro Siqueiros Mexican Painting: Frida Kahlo Learning Objectives Key Takeaways Key Points Frida Kahlo Paintings Influences Artistic Legacy \" , \"segment\": \"The Mexican Renaissance | Boundless Art <cover>History The Mexican Renaissance Mexican Muralism Mexican muralism can be defined as the mural painting that was used to promote nationalistic ideals as part of efforts to reunify the country under the post-Mexican Revolution government. Learning Objectives Compare and contrast the works of Diego Rivera, José Clemente Orozco, and David Siqueiros. Key Takeaways Key Points At the end of the Mexican Revolution in 1921, the government commissioned the painting of public murals as a way of promoting the ideals of the Revolution to a largely illiterate public. The murals were meant to glorify the Mexican Revolution, promote the identity of Mexico as a mestizo nation—</cover>celebrating both indigenous and Spanish backgrounds—and to promote art as a public commodity, not only the province of a few wealthy collectors. While Mexican Muralism was intended to promote the ideals of a new post revolution government, the artists were given complete freedom, so the imagery "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"15/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_14_1175329779#0_2438374238","relevance":3,"reason":"Dedicated art history article on Mexican Muralism with direct evidence that public murals promoted nationalistic and revolutionary ideals to a largely illiterate public, helped reunify post-revolution Mexico, promoted mestizo national identity, and treated art as a public commodity rather than an elite possession.","learned":"Mexican muralism affected social structure by helping reunify the country, educating the illiterate public, promoting mestizo identity, and democratizing art beyond wealthy collectors."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q149","reason":"This query is still producing direct grade-3 evidence about muralism's role in public education, reunification, nationalism, and social inclusion, so continue to the next result."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q149"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_243679954#2_552735355","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q149","rank":5,"snippet":"newly empowered <cover>people, and one of the ways it planned to do this was through art. A Government-backed program In 1920, the new government decided to follow in Dr. Atl's footsteps, and commissioned a large number of public works of art which would promote and support the values fundamental to the revolution and to help establish a new identity for Mexico. This new identity was based on Mexico's rich historical traditions as well as a sense of moving forward into the modern age. Importantly, most Mexicans at the time were illiterate, and promoting the new government's message could not be accomplished through traditional media such as pamphlets and newspapers. Instead, the government communicated their cause through large-scale murals in public places which could be seen by many. The murals' aesthetic appeal would also help Mexicans adapt to the new regime by affecting an overall sense of pride and cultural beauty within the communities as a whole.\" , \"start_char\": 1429.000000 , \"title\": \"Mexican Muralism - Concepts & Styles | TheArtStory\" , \"url\": \"https://www.theartstory.org/movement/mexican-muralism/history-</cover>and-concepts/"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"16/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_54_243679954#2_552735355","relevance":3,"reason":"Dedicated Mexican Muralism article with direct evidence that the post-revolutionary government used public murals to promote revolutionary values, establish a new Mexican identity, communicate with an illiterate population, and create pride and cultural beauty in communities.","learned":"Mexican murals affected social structure by communicating political and cultural values to ordinary citizens, helping legitimize a new regime, and fostering shared post-revolutionary national identity."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q149","reason":"The query is consistently producing grade-3 evidence about muralism's role in public education, nation-building, and social inclusion; continue to the next result."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q149"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_14_1175329779#3_2438380177","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q149","rank":6,"snippet":"<cover>Mexican muralism started a tradition that continues to this day in Mexico. In 1921, after the end of the Mexican Revolution, José Vasconcelos was appointed to head the Secretaría de Educación Pública. At the time, most of the Mexican population was illiterate and the government needed a way to promote the ideals of the Mexican Revolution. Vasconcelos helped establish a government-backed mural program, hiring the country’s best artists, for this purpose. Similar to mural use in the pre-Hispanic and colonial periods, the purpose of these murals were not simply aesthetic, but social, to promote certain ideals. These ideals or principles were to glorify the Mexican Revolution and the identity of Mexico as a mestizo nation, with the indigenous promoted as well as the Spanish. The movement was strongest from the 1920s to the 1950s, which corresponded to the country’s transformation from a mostly rural and mostly illiterate society to an industrialized one. While today they are part of Mexico’s identity, at the time they were controversial, especially those with socialist messages plastered on centuries-old colonial buildings.\" , \"start_char\": 2191.000000 , \"title\": \"The </cover>"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"17/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_14_1175329779#3_2438380177","relevance":3,"reason":"Dedicated art history content on Mexican muralism with direct evidence that murals had a social purpose: promoting revolutionary ideals, mestizo national identity, indigenous identity, and socialist messages to a mostly illiterate public during Mexico's transformation from rural/illiterate to industrialized society.","learned":"Mexican murals affected social structure by serving as state-backed public education and propaganda that promoted revolutionary, mestizo, indigenous, and socialist ideals during modernization."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q149","reason":"The current query is still returning direct grade-3 evidence about muralism's social purposes, public communication, and national identity effects; continue to harvest more relevant results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q149"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_14_1175329779#4_2438382302","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q149","rank":7,"snippet":"<cover>Mexican Muralism Los Tres Grandes Diego Rivera Jose Clemente Orozco David Alfaro Siqueiros Mexican Painting: Frida Kahlo Learning Objectives Key Takeaways Key Points Frida Kahlo Paintings Influences Artistic Legacy \" , \"segment\": \"Vasconcelos helped establish a government-backed mural program, hiring the country’s best artists, for this purpose. Similar to mural use in the pre-Hispanic and colonial periods, the purpose of these murals were not simply aesthetic, but social, to promote certain ideals. These ideals or principles were to glorify the Mexican Revolution and the identity of Mexico as a mestizo nation, with the indigenous promoted as well as the Spanish. The movement was strongest from the 1920s to the 1950s, which corresponded to the country’s transformation from a mostly rural and mostly illiterate society to an industrialized one. While today they are part of Mexico’s identity, at the time they were controversial, especially those with socialist messages plastered on centuries-old colonial buildings. One of the basic underpinnings of the nascence of a post-revolutionary Mexican art was that it should be public, available to the citizenry, and above all not the province of a few wealthy collectors. The great societal </cover>"}}
