{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ (+ vasconcelos \"secretaria de educacion publica\") (+ murals muralism) (+ masses people education identity))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_594901433#3_1283588358","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q153","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_594901433#3_1283588358\" , \"end_char\": 3750.000000 , \"headings\": \"The History of Mexico : Diego Rivera’s Murals at the National Palace The History of Mexico: Diego Rivera’s Murals at the National Palace How is history told? A new national identity Why murals? The North Wall The West Wall The South Wall An alternative history Notes: Additional resources: \" , \"segment\": \"José <cover>Vasconcelos, the new government’s Minister of Public Education, conceived of a collaboration between the government and artists. The result were state-sponsored murals </cover>such as those at the National Palace in Mexico City. Why murals? Rivera and other artists believed easel painting to be “aristocratic,” since for centuries this kind of art had been the purview of the elite. Instead they favored mural painting since it could present subjects on a large scale to a wide public audience. This idea—of directly addressing the people in public buildings—suited the muralists’ Communist politics. In 1922, Rivera (and others) signed the Manifesto of the Syndicate of Technical Workers, Painters, and Sculptors, arguing "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"23/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_594901433#3_1283588358","relevance":3,"reason":"Dedicated article on Diego Rivera’s National Palace murals with direct evidence that state-sponsored murals were used for a new national identity, rejected elite/aristocratic easel painting, addressed a wide public audience in public buildings, and aligned with Communist/social politics.","learned":"Mexican murals affected social structure by shifting art from elite private ownership to public communication for the people, supporting national identity and leftist social politics."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q153","reason":"This Vasconcelos/state-mural query is producing direct evidence on public education, elite-versus-mass art access, and national identity, so continue to find more grade-3 sources."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q153"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_135778196#13_149884004","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q153","rank":2,"snippet":"Representation in other media Gallery Paintings Murals Sculptures See also References Further reading External links \" , \"segment\": \"For then man will have thrown off all of his superstitions and irrational taboos.\\\" In his later years Rivera lived in the United States and Mexico. He died on November 24, 1957. Career in Mexico In 1920, urged by Alberto J. Pani, the Mexican ambassador to France, Rivera left France and traveled through Italy studying its art, including Renaissance frescoes. After José Vasconcelos became Minister of <cover>Education, Rivera returned to Mexico in 1921 to become involved in the government sponsored Mexican mural program planned by Vasconcelos. See also Mexican muralism. </cover>The program included such Mexican artists as José Clemente Orozco, David Alfaro Siqueiros, and Rufino Tamayo, and the French artist Jean Charlot. In January 1922, he painted – experimentally in encaustic – his first significant mural Creation in the Bolívar Auditorium of the National Preparatory School in Mexico City while guarding himself with a pistol against right-wing students. In the autumn of 1922, Rivera participated in the founding of the Revolutionary Union of Technical Workers, Painters and Sculptors, and later that year he joined the Mexican Communist Party (including "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"24/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_135778196#13_149884004","relevance":1,"reason":"This is mainly a Diego Rivera biographical passage. It mentions Rivera joining Vasconcelos's government-sponsored Mexican mural program and the Communist Party, but it does not directly explain how Mexican murals affected social structure.","learned":"Rivera participated in the Vasconcelos-backed mural program and later joined the Mexican Communist Party, but this result offers limited direct evidence on social effects."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q153","reason":"The Vasconcelos/state-mural query is still likely to return sources on public education, mass audiences, and national identity; continue to look for more direct evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q153"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_982614616#1_1775247061","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q153","rank":3,"snippet":"Diego Rivera's Murals Feature Diego Rivera's Murals Diego Rivera's Murals \" , \"segment\": \"Only a few years later his style changed. Inspired by the work of Cezanne he began to make Post-Impressionist paintings using simple shapes and vivid colors. His work began to attract more attention and some was exhibited. After spending some time studying in Italy, where he experimented with frescoes, Rivera returned to Mexico, where he became involved with a government mural program in 1921. The new Secretary of Public Education José <cover>Vasconcelos was an advocate of education through public art and he commissioned the creation of murals </cover>on several government buildings. Shortly after Rivera joined the Revolutionary Union of Technical Workers, Painters and Sculptors and he started to only paint his murals in fresco. Rivera believed that painting murals on the walls of public buildings made art accessible to the everyday man. His murals focused on telling stories that dealt with Mexican society and referenced the revolution of 1910. It featured large forms, bright colors and recurring images of farmers, laborers, popular Mexican figures and depictions of earth. He rose to international success with his vivid and beautiful compositions, "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"25/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_982614616#1_1775247061","relevance":3,"reason":"Dedicated feature on Diego Rivera's murals with direct evidence that Vasconcelos promoted education through public art, murals made art accessible to the everyday person, and Rivera's murals addressed Mexican society, the Revolution, farmers, laborers, and popular Mexican figures.","learned":"Mexican murals affected social structure by using public art as education, making art accessible beyond elites, and representing farmers and laborers as central figures in Mexican society."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q153","reason":"The Vasconcelos/state-sponsored mural query continues to find direct evidence on public education, accessibility, and representation of common people, so continue to the next result."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q153"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_982614616#0_1775245524","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q153","rank":4,"snippet":"leaders of the Mexican Mural Movement of the 1920s. A member of the Communist party, he created popular political murals throughout Mexico that often included attacks on the ruling class, the church and capitalism. Studying in Paris meant Rivera was exposed to different painting styles and movements. Initially his work was heavily influenced by Cubism, and Rivera met the Cubist master, Pablo Picasso in 1914. Only a few years later his style changed. Inspired by the work of Cezanne he began to make Post-Impressionist paintings using simple shapes and vivid colors. His work began to attract more attention and some was exhibited. After spending some time studying in Italy, where he experimented with frescoes, Rivera returned to Mexico, where he became involved with a government mural program in 1921. The new Secretary of Public Education José <cover>Vasconcelos was an advocate of education through public art and he commissioned the creation of murals </cover>on several government buildings.\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"Diego Rivera's Murals | History Detectives | PBS\" , \"url\": \"http://www.pbs.org/opb/historydetectives/feature/diego-riveras-murals/\" }"}}
