{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_14_1175329779#2_2438378444","relevance":3,"reason":"Dedicated art history article on Mexican Muralism with direct evidence that public murals carried nationalistic, social, and political messages, were part of efforts to reunify post-revolution Mexico, and helped promote revolutionary ideals to a largely illiterate population.","learned":"Mexican murals affected social structure by using public buildings to communicate social and political messages, reunify the country, and educate an illiterate public about revolutionary ideals."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q164","reason":"This Vasconcelos/illiterate-public query is producing direct grade-3 evidence on reunification, public education, revolutionary ideals, and identity formation; continue to harvest relevant results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q164"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_105840807#5_118791189","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q164","rank":3,"snippet":"became the Partido Revolucionario Institucional (PRI). During the Revolution, Atl supported the Carranza faction and promoted the work of Rivera, Orozco and Siqueiros, who would later be the founders of the muralism movement. Through the war and until 1921, Atl continued to paint murals among other activities including teaching the Mexico's next generation of artists and muralists. Mural movement Eagle and snake image from the Colegio San Ildefonso project by Jean Charlot. In 1921, after the end of the military phase of the Revolution, José Vasconcelos was appointed to head the Secretaría de Educación Pública. At the time, most of the Mexican population was <cover>illiterate and the government needed a way to promote the ideals of the Mexican Revolution. It was Vasconcelos's idea to have a government-backed mural program for this purpose. Similar to mural use in the pre Hispanic period and during the colonial period, the purpose of these murals </cover>were not simply aesthetic, but social, to promote certain ideals.\" , \"start_char\": 4459.000000 , \"title\": \"Mexican muralism - Wikipedia\" , \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mexican_muralism\" }"}}
