{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_105840807#5_118791189","relevance":3,"reason":"Dedicated Mexican muralism article with direct evidence that the government-backed mural program was created because most Mexicans were illiterate, and that the murals had a social purpose: promoting ideals of the Mexican Revolution rather than merely aesthetic goals.","learned":"Mexican murals affected social structure by serving as public education and social messaging for an illiterate population, spreading revolutionary ideals through state-sponsored public art."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q164","reason":"The Vasconcelos/illiterate-public query is yielding direct grade-3 evidence about public education, revolutionary ideals, and social purpose, so continue to the next result."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q164"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_433912029#6_872374026","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q164","rank":5,"snippet":"the landless indigenous working class. In 1920, José Vasconcelos, the newly-appointed Mexican Minister of Education, presented Rivera with a grant to fund a seventeen-month stay in Italy where he studied Etruscan, Byzantine, and Renaissance art. His exposure to Italian frescoes would prove to be influential to his later contribution to Mexico’s own mural tradition. In 1921, Rivera returned to Mexico for the first time since his departure in 1907. The post-revolutionary Mexican government was enacting a series of reforms to promote and solidify its nationalist and socialist <cover>ideals, and Vasconcelos recognized the power of murals for this purpose. He developed a government-sponsored public </cover>art program that commissioned artists to paint murals in government buildings throughout Mexico. Diego Rivera, David Alfaro Siqueiros, and José Clemente Orozco were among the most prominent of these artists and, together, became known as Los tres grandes (The Three Great Ones) for their contributions to Mexican art during this period. The public mural program promoted what was known as Mexican Modernism;\" , \"start_char\": 4031.000000 , \"title\": \"Diego Rivera – MMoCA\" , \"url\": \"https://www.mmoca.","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_105840807#6_118793425","rank":4,"relevance":3}]}}
